      body: "## Overview\n\nHydrapool is the 256 Foundation's pool server: a simple stratum server written in Rust with extensibility as the main goal, shipping solo and PPLNS modes, paying directly from the coinbase (no custody). It exists because ckpool is very hard to extend \u2014 interesting accounting/payout mechanisms mean wading through a decade of built-in proxy/node C code \u2014 and datum_gateway is simpler but tethered to the Ocean ecosystem [#2356 \xB7 2025-06-05 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]. Design goal: easy to run, easy to extend and experiment with, with 'lottery with friends' as a first-class use case [#2356 \xB7 2025-06-05 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#2358 \xB7 2025-06-05 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#2359 \xB7 2025-06-05 \xB7 szarka].\n\nIt is licensed AGPLv3, works with any bitcoin node that speaks bitcoin RPC, ships Prometheus/Grafana dashboards, and exposes an API so miners can download the PPLNS share accounting and check payouts themselves ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)). The Foundation's own framing is \"WordPress for pools\": a core platform with payouts as plug-ins (solo, PPLNS, and later Lightning or Ark), plus a P2Pool V2 path toward pooling with no operator at all ([256F projects page](../../raw/history/256f-org/256foundation-org-projects.md)). The Foundation says it has no plans to become a pool operator; the long-term goal is that users host their own instances ([newsletter, May 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-05-05-bitcoin-mining-will-not-be-decentralized-until-it-is-open-so.md)). Internally, Hydrapool uses P2Poolv2's stratum and accounting libraries ([jungly on X, 2026-05-20](../../raw/history/256f-research/2026-10-07-x-notable-jungly-hydrapool.md)).\n\nOn POD256 the hosts describe Hydrapool as one of the Foundation's four pillar projects (hashboard, control board, firmware, pool), funded through a grant to Jungly, who has a PhD in distributed systems and prior P2Pool work ([POD256 e090](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-15-e090.md); [POD256 e100](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-31-e100.md)). Eco's pitch is a one-click-deploy, self-hostable pool, on a VPS or at home, that friends can join, framed as resistance to future government attacks on mining tools ([POD256 e090](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-15-e090.md)); his April 2026 summary was that pools anyone can spin up trivially are hard for governments to shut down ([POD256 e112](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-15-e112.md)). His shorthand for the end state: Hydrapool plus P2Pool v2 equals decentralized pools ([POD256 e098](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-13-e098.md)). Grants for all four core projects were renewed on April 1, 2026, with Hydrapool's for three months, expandable with new funding ([POD256 e110](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-01-e110.md); [POD256 e111](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-10-e111.md)).\n\n## At a glance (As-Of 2026-10-10)\n\n| Item | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| License / language | AGPLv3, Rust | [README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md) |\n| Accounting modes | Solo and PPLNS (solo = a single user receiving 100% of PPLNS) | [README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md); [#3149 \xB7 2025-10-28 \xB7 econoalchemist] |\n| Payouts | Direct from the coinbase; operator never custodies funds | [README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md) |\n| Users per coinbase | 100 by default (see the Disputed note below) | [hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-home.md) |\n| Workers | Limited only by the operator's hardware | [README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md) |\n| Default ports | Stratum 3333, API 46884, Grafana 3000, Prometheus 9090 | [docker README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-docker-readme.md) |\n| Networks | `main`, `testnet4`, `signet` | [docker README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-docker-readme.md) |\n| Latest release | v2.6.0, published 2026-06-12 (but see the open issue #44 below) | [releases](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-releases.md) |\n\n## Design philosophy\n\n- Direct-from-coinbase payouts (Laurentia-style, no custody) agreed as the MVP; the direct-coinbase payout-count limit is a Bitmain firmware constraint \u2014 the trade-off is Antminer stock-firmware incompatibility [#1605 \xB7 2025-02-03 \xB7 econoalchemist] [#1607 \xB7 2025-02-03 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]. The newsletter puts that limit at some 16 addresses: an old stock Antminer will not handle a template with more payout addresses than that, while Hydrapool lets the operator configure the number ([newsletter, Sep 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-09-25-rig-bitcoin-mining-re-imagined.md)); POD256 described it as Bitmain's 15-16 address limit ([newsletter, Nov 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-11-24-assembling-freedom-11.md)).\n- Second concern: large coinbase payout lists eat blockspace \u2014 5,000 miners would consume meaningful blockspace, letting other pools net higher payouts [#1607 \xB7 2025-02-03 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- Target users: Bitaxe and Ember One owners who run hardware regardless of fee revenue; iterate later toward federation (fedipool), ecash (hashpool), or Lightning atomic swaps (p2poolv2) [#1608 \xB7 2025-02-03 \xB7 econoalchemist] [#1600 \xB7 2025-02-02 \xB7 econoalchemist]\n- Framed as the decentralization answer to big-pool pain points (ckpool-dev context) [#1711 \xB7 2025-03-11 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#1712 \xB7 2025-03-11 \xB7 econoalchemist]. The hydrapool.org rationale: pools are centralized, hard to set up and rarely open source, and governments could coerce them (KYC, censoring transactions); easily deployable pools let miners move hashrate away from such choke points ([hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-home.md)). See [Pool Centralization & FPPS](pool-centralization-and-fpps.md).\n- Hydrapool vs P2Poolv2 niches (jungly): Hydrapool = run a pool for yourself/your network, hackable accounting and payouts; P2Poolv2 = opinionated, decentralized pool built to scale; they share core libs (stratum, accounting, coinbase) which live in the P2Poolv2 repo [#3915 \xB7 2025-12-10 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#3917 \xB7 2025-12-10 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- Bring-your-own-node philosophy: connecting your own node is a key feature; node install stays out of scope because sync times are an ordeal, and it keeps Hydrapool independent of node versions [#3155 \xB7 2025-10-28 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- Auditability over server-side trust: rather than asking users to trust a share database, the pool exposes validated shares through an API so anyone can audit the PPLNS window, with minimal persistent storage to survive crashes and restarts ([newsletter, Sep 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-09-25-rig-bitcoin-mining-re-imagined.md)).\n- Long-term goals listed on hydrapool.org: alternative payout models such as echash, talking to other Hydra Pool instances via P2Poolv2, encrypted server/client communication and reduced bandwidth ([hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-home.md)).\n\n> **Status: Disputed** \u2014 pruned nodes for mining templates\n> jungly initially said Bitcoin Core doesn't support getblocktemplate on pruned nodes [#2759 \xB7 2025-08-06 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]; Skot pushed back \u2014 pruned nodes keep the full validated UTXO set, which should suffice for templates [#2761 \xB7 2025-08-06 \xB7 Skot Bitaxe] [#2763 \xB7 2025-08-07 \xB7 Skot Bitaxe]; jungly conceded he might be right and wants to test [#2764 \xB7 2025-08-07 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]; datapoint: szarka mines on OCEAN with multiple pruned Knots nodes, no issues [#2776 \xB7 2025-08-14 \xB7 szarka]. Best assessment as of 2026-10-08: pruned-node mining works in practice (szarka's OCEAN setup), formal support question still worth testing.\n\n## Architecture history: ckpool fork to Rust on P2Poolv2 libs\n\n1. **ckpool fork (April 2025).** The first stack (PR #12, 2025-04-10) used docker compose to run bitcoind for signet/testnet4, a ckpool-solo fork that always paid the pool address, a p2poolv2 node capturing shares for flexible payouts, and cpuminer for development ([PR #12](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-10-github-hydrapool-pr-12-add-dockerfiles-to-build-hydrapool-so.md)). The public Telehash #2 build paid the 256 Foundation address automatically (any vanity username, no BTC address) and used a dashboard forked from CKstats; it ran on a bare-metal server in Florida with 64 cores and 128 GB of RAM ([newsletter, May 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-05-05-bitcoin-mining-will-not-be-decentralized-until-it-is-open-so.md)).\n2. **Rewrite decision (May\u2013July 2025).** Back-end issues forced roughly nine server restarts in the first hours of Telehash #2 ([newsletter, Jun 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-06-19-you-know-i-m-something-of-a-decentralized-pool-myself.md)). After reviewing Stratum v2 and DATUM, the team chose to write a stratum server from scratch in Rust; benchmarks against CK Pool were comparable ([newsletter, Jul 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-07-15-the-bigger-they-are-the-harder-they-fall.md)). The early ckpool-extension plans (issues #9\u2013#11: PPLNS, coinbase-only payouts) were closed because the p2pool library's accounting crate and payout mechanism replaced them ([issue #9](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-09-github-hydrapool-issue-9-add-pplns-share-accounting-support.md); [issue #10](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-09-github-hydrapool-issue-10-payouts-directly-from-coinbase-onl.md); [issue #11](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-09-github-hydrapool-issue-11-explore-ckpool-s-pplns-payout-mana.md)).\n3. **Compatibility testing on signet (Jul\u2013Sep 2025).** A new test server on signet (`test.hydrapool.org:3333`) was used to validate many miner types, because there is no Stratum v1 standard ([newsletter, Aug 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-08-18-is-open-source-communism.md)). Over 30 different kinds of miners connected and exposed stratum-handling issues that were fixed ([newsletter, Sep 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-09-25-rig-bitcoin-mining-re-imagined.md)). The maintainer chose signet over testnet4 because of the state testnet4 was in ([issue #7](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-09-github-hydrapool-issue-7-deploy-for-testnet4-to-enable-testi.md)).\n4. **Binaries, then Docker again (Oct\u2013Nov 2025).** On 2025-10-23 pool2win said the project had moved away from ckpool and would ship prebuilt binaries rather than run the pool in Docker ([PR #19](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-08-29-github-hydrapool-pr-19-build-add-dockerfile-for-hydra-pool.md)); a Debian package workflow (cargo-deb) arrived in 1.1.9, and by 1.1.14 docker compose (with cosign-signed images) became the primary way to run the pool while the Ansible templates were deprecated ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)).\n5. **Storage and dashboards.** Shares are stored with p2pool's store crate on RocksDB ([issue #8](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-09-github-hydrapool-issue-8-store-all-shares-generated-by-miner.md)); the planned ckpool-style dashboard and per-username filtering were replaced by Grafana dashboards ([issue #4](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-09-github-hydrapool-issue-4-ckpool-like-dashboard-for-total-has.md); [issue #6](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-09-github-hydrapool-issue-6-add-support-to-filter-by-username-f.md)).\n6. **P2Poolv2 libraries.** Each 2.x release bumps the shared libs (for example 2.4.0 moved to p2poolv2 v0.7.0 and 2.5.0 to v0.10.12) ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)); the mode logic now lives in those libs, which is where issue #44 originates (see Known issues).\n\n### Origin story as told on POD256 (2025-08 \u2192 2026-08)\n\n- **Why build it.** Scott said one motivation was that running Public Pool plus a Bitcoin node on a Raspberry Pi was very painful ([POD256 e124](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-08-17-e124.md)); Eco's own attempt on an old Raspberry Pi 4 took about sixteen days just to sync the blockchain and the hardware failed after a couple of weeks ([POD256 e090](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-15-e090.md)).\n- **CKPool fork, then a rewrite.** The first Hydrapool was a CKPool fork that ran Telehash #2 (Cinco de Mayo, Austin); lessons from that event sent the team back to the drawing board, and Jungly rebuilt it from scratch as a basic Stratum server in Rust ([POD256 e090](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-15-e090.md)). Before the rewrite the team reviewed CKPool (C), OCEAN's DATUM and the SRI Stratum v2 implementation ([POD256 e082](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-08-27-e082.md)). By January 2026 Eco described it as not a direct fork of CKPool but written from the ground up in Rust, with many design decisions inspired by Con Kolivas's CKPool ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)); Scott noted most pools, Hydrapool's design included, drew on CKPool's code ([POD256 e111](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-10-e111.md)).\n- **Design goal.** A vanilla, modular Rust Stratum server, so experimenters can try payout mechanisms without reimplementing Stratum, with near one-click deployment and a config file naming the reward address ([POD256 e082](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-08-27-e082.md)).\n- **Vardiff.** Signet testing found a miner's best difficulty stuck at a dead even 100,000 because the server was not adjusting share difficulty; Hydrapool mostly follows CKPool's vardiff approach rewritten in Rust ([POD256 e084](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-09-07-e084.md)). By late August 2025 difficulty negotiation was solved and Jungly was implementing PPLNS ([POD256 e082](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-08-27-e082.md)).\n- **Stratum test logs.** The signet test server logged how each miner's Stratum client connects (message order, machine, firmware) with a colour-coded status table; miners pointed any vanity username at it for about five minutes, and per-hardware logs were downloadable with IP addresses hidden ([POD256 e082](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-08-27-e082.md); [POD256 e084](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-09-07-e084.md)). Jungly presented this testing at Baltic Honey Badger in Riga (same source). Pools also receive each miner's user agent on connect, and the published logs had IP addresses removed ([POD256 e092](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-29-e092.md)).\n- **Release.** Expected around the start of October 2025, with PPLNS share accounting and difficulty adjustment done ([POD256 e088](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-09-24-e088.md)); Jungly technically published the first GitHub release around POD256 e091, and a walkthrough found a couple of bugs ([POD256 e091](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-22-e091.md)).\n\n## Running your own instance (operator how-to)\n\n### What you need\n\n- A bitcoin node with RPC and ZMQ block notifications (`zmqpubhashblock`, e.g. `tcp://0.0.0.0:28334` on the node; inside containers use `host.docker.internal`, not localhost) ([docker README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-docker-readme.md)). The node is bring-your-own; the reference walk-through builds a pool server on an old Dell Optiplex 9020 with Ubuntu Server 24.04.3 LTS, Bitcoin Core from Snap with chain data on a 2TB auxiliary drive, and the Hydrapool Docker files ([hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-home.md)).\n- Linux. v2.5.3 removed the Windows and Mac builds ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)); the README still advertises Linux, Windows and MacOS binaries and is out of date on this point ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)).\n\n### Install methods\n\n| Method | How | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Docker (recommended) | Download `docker-compose.yml` and `config-example.toml` from the latest release; `docker compose -f docker-compose.yml up` | Starts the pool plus Prometheus/Grafana; images are cosign-signed and verifiable against the github.com/256foundation certificate identity |\n| Binaries | `hydrapool-installer.sh` from the releases page | Installs `hydrapool` (runs the pool) and `hydrapool_cli` (query state, generate auth tokens); Docker still recommended for the dashboards |\n| Source | `cargo build --release` | Needs Rust 1.88.0 or newer; on Ubuntu 22.04 the distro Rust may need replacing and `libssl-dev`, `pkg-config`, `clang`, `libclang-dev` may be required |\n\nAll rows: [README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md).\n\nInstall notes from POD256:\n\n- **Why Docker.** The team chose Docker containers for the pool and dashboard over a Debian package as the easiest to maintain with the broadest coverage, with step-by-step guides for laptop/desktop, VPS and compile-from-source; Start9/Umbrel packaging was left to others. Prometheus is the database, Grafana the display ([POD256 e092](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-29-e092.md)).\n- **How many steps.** Prerequisites are a Bitcoin node and Docker; setup is roughly two commands to fetch two configuration files, edit a few parameters and update bitcoin.conf to match ([POD256 e097](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-03-e097.md)). Eco's hydrapool.org guide (old Dell Optiplex, Ubuntu Server, Bitcoin Core via Snap \u2014 any node with RPC works, e.g. Knots) adds generating a secure password into the config files before opening the pool to the public ([POD256 e100](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-31-e100.md)).\n- **Dashboards.** The Docker install includes Prometheus and Grafana; building from source means setting those up separately. Grafana is on local port 3,000. A dashboard reset after an apt upgrade turned out to be display-only \u2014 the share database was intact ([POD256 e098](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-13-e098.md)).\n- **Gotcha.** Eco broke Grafana's data feed after deleting an accidentally created `prometheus.yml` directory while adding a salted password hash to secure the server ([POD256 e099](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-17-e099.md)).\n- **One-click cloud deploy.** Schnitzel and Jungly were building an Ansible one-click deployment of Hydrapool plus D++'s HashDash front end for a VPS ([POD256 e103](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-02-04-e103.md)); by April 2026 Eco said Schnitzel had built an Ansible package and one-click AWS deployment ([POD256 e112](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-15-e112.md)). Note the CHANGELOG had deprecated the earlier Ansible templates in 1.1.14 (see Architecture history).\n- **Example home instance.** Eco's test instance ran on an old Dell Optiplex with Ubuntu Server plus a Bitcoin node, at about 12 Terahash from five workers ([POD256 e097](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-03-e097.md)).\n\n### config.toml essentials\n\n- Minimum edits: bitcoin RPC URL and credentials, `zmqpubhashblock`, `network`, and `bootstrap_address` (which defaults to the 256 Foundation's donation address) ([docker README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-docker-readme.md)). Since 2.0.0 the default mainnet config uses the Foundation's address ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)), so change it.\n- `bootstrap_address` receives the reward if a block is found in roughly the first 10-seconds or so after the server boots ([hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-home.md)).\n- Optional donation and operator-fee percentages are in basis points (100 = 1%); both are off by default. `pool_signature` (the coinbase tag) can be changed from the default \"hydrapool\", max 16 bytes ([hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-home.md)); leave it blank to not identify yourself [#4437 \xB7 2026-02-17 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}].\n- Miners can pass a starting difficulty (`d=`) and a hashrate hint (`h=`) in the stratum password field, alongside suggest-difficulty, so a 1 TH/s Bitaxe and a 1 EH/s fleet can connect without manual tuning ([newsletter, May 2026](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2026-05-28-assembling-freedom-27.md)); the feature was on the April 2026 roadmap ([newsletter, Apr 2026](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2026-04-11-assembling-freedom-23.md)).\n\n### bitcoin.conf: Docker networking\n\nThe compose file runs Hydrapool on an isolated bridged network, so the node must accept RPC from it ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)):\n\n```ini\n# node on the same host as Docker\nrpcbind=0.0.0.0\nrpcallowip=172.16.0.0/12\n```\n\n- Containers reach the host as `host.docker.internal`. If the node is on another host, point the RPC URL there and allow the Docker host's IP, because container traffic is NATed ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)).\n- Symptom of getting this wrong (resolved): a Docker network missing from `rpcallowip` made bitcoind return 403 Forbidden and Hydrapool panicked with a misleading JSON-parse error; p2poolv2 PR 251 (merged 2025-11-04) now logs bitcoind's HTTP status and message ([issue #32](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-11-03-github-hydrapool-issue-32-better-error-messages-are-needed-w.md)).\n- Services wait for full bitcoind sync before starting; a support request was logged to point at an existing bitcoind and multiple nodes for redundancy [#1995 \xB7 2025-05-02 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#1996 \xB7 2025-05-02 \xB7 Bartholomew]; 2.5.9 stops waiting for chain sync in hydrapool mode ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)).\n\n### blockmaxweight: leave room for the coinbase\n\nEvery payout output costs block weight. If regular transactions fill the default 4,000,000 WU, Bitcoin Core may reject the template; set `blockmaxweight` lower ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)):\n\n| P2PKH outputs in coinbase | Approx. coinbase weight | Suggested `blockmaxweight` |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 20 | ~2,928 WU | `3997000` |\n| 100 | ~13,808 WU | `3986000` |\n| 200 | ~27,408 WU | `3972500` |\n| 500 | ~68,208 WU | `3930000` |\n| 1000 | ~136,208 WU | `3860000` |\n\nSegWit outputs need slightly less room ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)).\n\n> **Status: Disputed** \u2014 is 100 users a hard limit?\n> The README says the pool only accommodates up to 100 users for coinbase and block weight reasons ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md); repeated in the [Nov 2025 newsletter](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-11-24-assembling-freedom-11.md)). hydrapool.org says 100 unique users in the coinbase is a default that can be raised via the blockmaxweight instructions ([hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-home.md)), and the README's own table goes to 1000 outputs. Best assessment: 100 is the shipped default, not a protocol limit; beyond it, operators trade block space (and fee revenue) for payout count, and old stock Antminers cap out far lower (see Design philosophy).\n\n### Securing a public server\n\n- API auth: generate `auth_user`/`auth_token` with `docker compose run --rm hydrapool-cli gen-auth <USERNAME> <PASSWORD>`, paste into config.toml, then put the same credentials in a custom `prometheus.yml` and in the `docker-compose.yml` healthcheck ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)).\n- Default credentials are Grafana admin/admin and Prometheus hydrapool/hydrapool \u2014 change both before exposing them ([docker README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-docker-readme.md); [README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)).\n- Put nginx in front of public dashboards and the API ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)).\n\n### Share-audit API\n\n`/pplns_shares` downloads every PPLNS share the pool tracks, with optional `start_time`/`end_time` in RFC3339; `/health` is the health check ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)). The API returned an empty array on 2.2.2 because PPLNS share saving was switched off during the January 2026 telehash to avoid slowdowns; the maintainer planned to restore it as a crate feature ([issue #40](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-02-03-github-hydrapool-issue-40-pplns-api-returns-empty-array.md)).\n\nWhy it is designed this way (POD256): rather than make novice operators store and serve a full share database, the pool streams validated shares in real time so interested miners can keep their own database and audit fairness ([POD256 e090](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-15-e090.md); [POD256 e087](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-09-17-e087.md)); Ryan proposed Mujina emit a standard share log to compare against the pool's (same source). The hosts contrasted this with Slush Pool's old documented verification method and OCEAN's API, which is available on request ([POD256 e094](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-12-e094.md)). Archived shares let users audit an operator's accounting, though the payout itself still requires trust in the operator ([POD256 e099](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-17-e099.md)).\n\nRetention: Eco said Hydrapool retains worker data for about seven days in Prometheus, after which it drops off ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)); in September 2026 a host described about a two-week or fifteen-day rolling history in the Foundation instance's Prometheus/Grafana logs ([POD256 e125](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-09-07-e125.md)). Both are recollections on air; retention is set by the Prometheus configuration, so archive the share API yourself if you need history.\n\n### Upgrades\n\n`docker compose pull` then `docker compose up -d --force-recreate`. Going from v1.x.x to v2.x.x or higher needs a one-time database reset (`docker compose down -v`) ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md)), because 2.0.0 switched to Bitcoin-compatible share serialization and shipped no migration script ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)).\n\n## Payout semantics\n\n- The pool is always in PPLNS mode \u2014 a single user simply receives 100% of the shares; add a second username/address and the coinbase starts splitting; multiple of your own addresses all share the coinbase [#3149 \xB7 2025-10-28 \xB7 econoalchemist] [#3150 \xB7 2025-10-28 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- Solo-mode semantics: connect as a single user (any number of workers) and all coinbase goes to that user \u2014 no config change needed; an explicit solo_address option considered; the payout-file work also enables single- or multi-address lotto setups, but not multiuser solo [#3960 \xB7 2025-12-15 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#3961 \xB7 2025-12-15 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#3965 \xB7 2025-12-15 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#3969 \xB7 2025-12-15 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#3975 \xB7 2025-12-15 \xB7 AgentP]\n- Telehash mode: with donation at 100%, address validation and PPLNS accounting are skipped so non-address usernames work (2.1.4\u20132.1.6) ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)).\n- Custom payout lists: payout_file_path in the p2poolv2 config makes the coinbase builder load payouts from a file (<100 lines, falls back to PPLNS on any error), while Hydrapool fetches from a downstream_payout_url API \u2014 ultimate flexibility for reward-sharing schemes like boot.gridlabs.science; tradeoff: that instance becomes custodial by choice [#3928 \xB7 2025-12-12 \xB7 AgentP] [#3930 \xB7 2025-12-12 \xB7 AgentP] [#3933 \xB7 2025-12-12 \xB7 AgentP] [#3946 \xB7 2025-12-13 \xB7 AgentP]\n\n- POD256 framing: Hydrapool always runs PPLNS \u2014 a solo instance with one address gets 100% of shares, a shared instance splits proportionally ([POD256 e092](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-29-e092.md)). The two use cases Eco names are solo mode for self-hosting and a shared pool such as a hackerspace hosting for its members ([POD256 e090](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-15-e090.md); [POD256 e086](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-09-10-e086.md)). Outside a Telehash it works like CKPool solo (finder gets the whole reward) or as PPLNS shared mining, and an operator can take a cut \u2014 Eco noted CKPool solo takes 2% ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)).\n- Username: the Stratum username must be a valid Bitcoin address; during a Telehash all rewards go to the Foundation regardless of the address entered, which the dashboard frames as hashrate donations ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)).\n- Fees and donations: the optional operator fee is taken only when a block is found ([POD256 e094](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-12-e094.md)); self-hosted Hydrapool has an optional donation setting (Eco's example: 2% of shares to the Foundation) that defaults to zero ([POD256 e097](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-03-e097.md)).\n- Coinbase payout count (POD256): default up to 100 users, user-adjustable ([POD256 e093](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-05-e093.md); [POD256 e094](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-12-e094.md)). Eco frames this as breaking the Bitmain-induced industry limit of roughly fifteen or sixteen addresses ([POD256 e097](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-03-e097.md)); he warned stock Antminers should not be pointed at an instance exceeding the firmware's cap ([POD256 e094](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-12-e094.md)), and in April 2026 the hosts said Hydrapool deliberately does not cater to Bitmain's limits ([POD256 e112](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-15-e112.md)). What a stock Antminer actually does with a 100-address template was still an open question in January 2026 ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)). Coinbase size is bounded in theory only by block size, which is why Hydrapool exposes the max block weight setting (Jungly's calculation is in the README) ([POD256 e094](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-12-e094.md)). See [Pool Centralization & FPPS](pool-centralization-and-fpps.md#the-bitmain-coinbase-output-cap).\n\n### PPLNS window sizing (storage)\n\nFor a family-and-friends hashrate-heating pool where some miners run only in winter, a `pplns_ttl_days` above 365 would still reward past work if a block hits in summer. pool2win's estimate: about 100 users need 90-100 GB of disk for a year of shares; the cost scales with users (BTC addresses), not workers, so friends should reuse one address across their miners ([issue #27](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-10-30-github-hydrapool-issue-27-pplns-ttl-days.md)). See [Pool Choice for Heat Miners](pool-choice-for-heat-miners.md).\n\n### Plugin system (planned)\n\nPR #42 (2026-03-19) proposed overriding PPLNS payouts with a local payout file and forwarding high-difficulty shares to an external pool ([PR #42](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-03-19-github-hydrapool-pr-42-custom-user-defined-payouts.md)). Rather than merge that into `main.rs`, the maintainer opened issue #43 for a payout/accounting plugin system: Rust crates or `.so` plugins enabled from config.toml, traits such as `fill_distribution_from_shares` and `build_coinbase_transaction`, limited RocksDB access, a score-based PPLNS plugin as default and `simple_pplns` moved into a plugin ([issue #43](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-03-19-github-hydrapool-issue-43-build-payout-plugin-system.md)). The April 2026 roadmap also lists a gamified dashboard and Lightning payouts ([newsletter, Apr 2026](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2026-04-05-pod256-episode-110-newsletter-april-fools-real-progress-open.md)).\n\n### Roadmap as discussed on POD256 (2025-12 \u2192 2026-08)\n\n- **Roadmap items (March 2026):** Ansible one-click deploy, a plugin system, Start9 and Umbrel packages, Lightning and eCash payouts ([POD256 e109](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-03-25-e109.md)); eCash-based payouts like Hashpool had been floated in December 2025 ([POD256 e100](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-31-e100.md)).\n- **Sequence (April 2026):** step one is lowering the barrier to running a pool; Start9 and Umbrel packaging next; later, P2Pool v2 integration so multiple Hydrapool instances can coordinate ([POD256 e112](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-15-e112.md)).\n- **April 2026 work:** accept a starting difficulty in the Stratum password field (as NiceHash sends during a Telehash), improve error handling and logging, and benchmark against CKPool on identical hardware ([POD256 e111](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-10-e111.md)). The proposed convention adds a new `h=` hashrate parameter next to `d=` so the server computes difficulty \u2014 no known server supported the hashrate form at the time (same source).\n- **Packaging status.** Community pull requests to package Hydrapool for StartOS arrived in November 2025 ([POD256 e095](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-19-e095.md)); by July 2026 community member Karpuzz Mining had packaged it for Umbrel and Jungly was working on Start9 and Umbrel packaging ([POD256 e121](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-07-27-e121.md)).\n- **Hashrate splitting.** Exergy splits 5% of its office heaters' hashrate to the Foundation via an OCEAN DATUM split; a Hydrapool proxy mode to split hashrate without DATUM was discussed ([POD256 e102](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-30-e102.md)). Iko floated changing the Foundation pool's coinbase so the Foundation takes 50% and PPLNS splits the rest, like Parasite Pool ([POD256 e107](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-03-11-e107.md)).\n- **GridPool fork.** Agent P forked Hydrapool (and P2Pool v2) so Hydrapool submits shares to a local GridPool node that supplies the payout list; a PR to Hydrapool was open with Jungly preferring a slightly different approach ([POD256 e116](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-06-10-e116.md)). This is the same work as PR #42 above.\n- **Miner-model breakdown.** Scott suggested that since miners send a user agent on connect, Hydrapool could expose a miner-model breakdown via Prometheus/API ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)).\n\n### Hydrapool and P2Pool v2\n\nJungly works on both: P2Pool v2 replaces centralized share accounting with a separate share chain where block rewards are proportional to shares in a window, and Hydrapool is to be its Stratum server component ([POD256 e086](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-09-10-e086.md)); multiple Hydrapool instances could network over that share chain (same source). Eco in January 2026: Hydrapool is the Stratum server component, P2Pool v2 the share-chain component, and the roadmap is to make them work together ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)). In April 2026 Localhost raised money to fund a full-time contributor on P2Pool v2 alongside Jungly, who continues Hydrapool work ([POD256 e112](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-15-e112.md)). See [Decentralized Pool Designs](../protocols/decentralized-pool-designs.md).\n\n## Stratum V2\n\n- Declined SRI crates (2025-12-10): the maintainer said SRI did more than Hydrapool needed, carried technical debt and had tightly coupled APIs; he planned to add SV2 as devices adopt it, starting with encrypted/compressed channels ([issue #37](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-12-10-github-hydrapool-issue-37-use-rust-sv2-crates.md)).\n- Issue #41 (average-gary, 2026-02-17) proposes native SV2 Mining Protocol support on the refactored `stratum-core` crate \u2014 no Job Declaration or Template Distribution \u2014 with SV1 on port 3333 and SV2 on 3334 behind an opt-in `sv2` cargo feature. An end-to-end testnet4 run on 2026-03-04 (Bitaxe \u2192 SRI Translator Proxy \u2192 Hydrapool, Noise NX on port 3334) had 100+ shares accepted with zero rejections. Interop lessons: send every job as a future job activated by SetNewPrevHash; send all NewMiningJob messages before any SetNewPrevHash in a batch; don't send standard-channel broadcasts to extended-channel-only connections ([issue #41](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-02-17-github-hydrapool-issue-41-feat-stratum-v2-mining-protocol-su.md)).\n- The 256 Foundation joined the Stratum V2 Working Group, citing server-side SV2 in Hydrapool and client-side SV2 in Mujina ([256F on X, 2026](../../raw/history/256f-research/2026-10-07-x-256foundation-2026.md)). See [Stratum in Practice](../protocols/stratum-in-practice.md).\n\n## Hardware compatibility\n\nhydrapool.org/hardware-tests asks owners of untested hardware to point at `stratum+tcp://test.hydrapool.org:3333` for 5 to 10 minutes while server logs are watched. The capture lost the green/yellow/red pass/fail colours, so the table below is the set of rows that name a firmware (the ones evidently tested); the other ~100 rows are nameplate specs ([hardware tests](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-hardware-tests-html.md)).\n\n| Make / model | Firmware | Hash power (nameplate) | Efficiency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bitaxe Gamma | ESP Miner v2.7.1 | 1.2 Th/s | 15 J/Th |\n| Bitaxe Supra | ESP Miner v2.7.1 | 0.8 Th/s | 17.5 J/Th |\n| Futurebit Apollo I | Apollo API v2.0.6 | 3.5 Th/s | 45 J/Th |\n| Ember One 00 | test firmware | 3 Th/s | 33.3 J/Th |\n\nField notes:\n\n- Multi-firmware test day (August 2025) surfaced parsing bugs: Whatsminers sending username \"None\"; Antminer firmware's subscribe messages formatted in an unanticipated JSON shape breaking parsing; commitment made to publish the message formats each firmware sends [#2751 \xB7 2025-08-06 \xB7 csh2000] [#2753 \xB7 2025-08-06 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#2755 \xB7 2025-08-06 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- Client compatibility: some older Bitaxe firmware versions don't play nice with Hydra Pool \u2014 update to latest ESP-Miner release; a Whatsminer M64 wouldn't fully power up or submit shares to Hydra but booted fine on the regular pool [#2740 \xB7 2025-08-05 \xB7 econoalchemist] [#2747 \xB7 2025-08-05 \xB7 Dylan Seib]\n- \"Share validation failed: Insufficient work\" on V1.1.5 (Knots 29.1, an AvalonMiner 1246 and a Bitaxe) was fixed in v1.1.6 ([issue #21](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-10-25-github-hydrapool-issue-21-share-validation-failed-insufficie.md)).\n- Invalid-address authorize: Hydrapool originally closed the connection silently (Public Pool returns error 20; ckpool returns result false). p2poolv2 PR 312 (2026-01-12) adopted ckpool's two-strike policy \u2014 first failure returns -401 with a reason, second disconnects ([issue #35](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-11-16-github-hydrapool-issue-35-mining-authorize-with-an-invalid-b.md)). The same log shows a Bitaxe sending `mining.configure` with mask ffffffff and Hydrapool answering version-rolling with mask 1fffe000.\n- Hydrapool sends `result`/`error` as null in responses, after the same omission broke Bitaxe on Miningcore pools ([ESP-Miner #916](../../raw/firmware/esp-miner/2025-05-12-github-esp-miner-issue-916-incompatibility-with-miningcore-b.md)).\n- Duplicate shares are detected and rejected since 2.1.0 ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)).\n\n## Known issues (As-Of 2026-10-10)\n\n> **Status: Open bug** \u2014 v2.6.0 ignores `mode = \"hydrapool\"` (issue #44, opened 2026-10-04)\n> The official v2.6.0 binary always builds the sharechain PPLNS payout; its StratumServerBuilder never sets the mode. On testnet4, 99% of the coinbase went to the genesis share's unspendable NUMS address and every share was rejected as low difficulty; the reporter warns a mainnet block found this way would burn almost all of its reward. He traces it to v2.5.3 (\"Update to latest p2poolv2 libs\") and says the 2.5.9 ASERT-difficulty fix has no effect in the binary for the same reason. A build of `p2poolv2_node` from v0.12.0 with the mode passed correctly paid exactly 1% to the fee address and split the rest 60/40 between two miners by shares ([issue #44](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-10-04-github-hydrapool-issue-44-v2-6-0-ignores-mode-hydrapool-coin.md)). Operators on v2.5.3 or later should test on testnet4/signet and inspect the coinbase before mining mainnet.\n\n- Memory footprint: field report of an old instance at 13.4 GB RAM with 50,000 open sockets (socket leak, several-months-old version) vs the production instance running version 2.5.8 stable at around 1GB of memory usage; fix submitted as PR #605 to p2poolv2 [#5055 \xB7 2026-07-20 \xB7 AgentP] [#5059 \xB7 2026-07-21 \xB7 AgentP] [#5058 \xB7 2026-07-21 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#5063 \xB7 2026-07-21 \xB7 AgentP]\n- 2.5.5 fixed u64 saturation of the total-valid-shares counter in the Prometheus output ([CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)).\n- Leaderboard uses a rolling window \u2014 old >1 GH scores flush out [#4623 \xB7 2026-03-18 \xB7 szarka] [#4624 \xB7 2026-03-18 \xB7 econoalchemist]\n- Dashboard anomaly (2026-02-02): the 256F hydrapool dash overstates hashrates \u2014 'my humble underclocked S9 putting out 15.5 and up to 19 Th/s whereas the braiinsOS dashboard shows closer to 10. Same with my Bitaxe, it's definitely not putting out 2.2 Th/s' \u2014 flagged as a dash or pool estimation bug [#4380 \xB7 2026-02-02 \xB7 PizzAndy]\n- Scope guard: Hydrapool is a BTC pool \u2014 BC3 (bitcoinIII)/sha3t mining attempts get 'Share rejected (low difficulty share)' [#5459 \xB7 2026-09-06 \xB7 Rand Childs]\n\n## Release timeline\n\nGitHub lists 35 releases, all stable, from v1.0.1 (2025-10-22) to v2.6.0 (2026-06-12) ([release history](../../raw/history/256f-research/2026-10-07-github-release-history-key-repos.md)). Publish dates below are from the releases page; changelog dates sometimes differ (e.g. the changelog dates 2.0.1 and 2.0.0 2025-12-24 and 2.4.0 2026-01-24) ([releases](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-releases.md); [CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)).\n\n| Version | Published | Notable changes |\n|---|---|---|\n| v1.0.1 | 2025-10-22 | First GitHub release |\n| v1.1.9 | 2025-10-29 | Debian package workflow (cargo-deb) |\n| v1.1.13 | 2025-10-30 | Dockerfiles for Hydrapool, Grafana, Prometheus; docker compose file |\n| v1.1.18 | 2025-10-31 | Follows 1.1.14: cosign-signed images, docker compose as the primary run method, Ansible deprecated |\n| v2.0.1 | 2025-12-26 | 2.0.0 BREAKING: Bitcoin-compatible share serialization (wipe store.db); coinbase distribution in Grafana; Foundation address as default mainnet config |\n| v2.1.0 | 2026-01-09 | Duplicate-share rejection; load-test mode ignoring difficulty |\n| v2.1.3 | 2026-01-12 | ckpool-style two-strike authorize failure |\n| v2.1.4 \u2013 v2.1.6 | 2026-01-15 | 100%-donation (telehash) mode skips address validation and PPLNS |\n| v2.1.7 | 2026-01-16 | Stop saving chain and PPLNS shares for now (performance) |\n| v2.1.9 | 2026-01-18 | Graceful termination (removes Grafana spikes on restart) |\n| v2.2.0 | 2026-01-19 | Faster notify broadcast; clean up inactive users/workers |\n| v2.4.0 | 2026-02-16 | 2.3.0 yanked; p2poolv2 v0.7.0; used for the February 2026 telehash |\n| v2.5.3 | 2026-05-14 | Windows and Mac builds removed (2.5.0: p2poolv2 libs v0.10.12) |\n| v2.5.5 | 2026-05-16 | u64 saturation fix in Prometheus output |\n| v2.5.8 | 2026-05-19 | Production 256F instance ran 2.5.8 in July 2026 |\n| v2.6.0 | 2026-06-12 | Latest; see issue #44 |\n\nSources for the table rows: [CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md); [release history](../../raw/history/256f-research/2026-10-07-github-release-history-key-repos.md); [#4427 \xB7 2026-02-16 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]; [#5058 \xB7 2026-07-21 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}].\n\n## History\n\n- 2025-03-27: the Foundation described Hydra Pool as a one-click deployable pool package with Stratum v2 support, built for the complete Ember One system ([256F on X, 2025 H1](../../raw/history/256f-research/2026-10-07-x-256foundation-2025-h1.md)). POD256 #068 pitched it as \"The Wordpress of Mining Pools\" ([POD256 #068](../../raw/history/pod256/2025-04-17-pod256-068-the-wordpress-of-mining-pools-with-jungly.md)).\n- 2025-04-28: early iteration opened to the community for testing at stratum+tcp://stratum.hydrapool.org:3333 \u2014 any username works, payouts configured to the 256 Foundation address; stats at stats.hydrapool.org [#1889 \xB7 2025-04-28 \xB7 econoalchemist] [#1891 \xB7 2025-04-28 \xB7 econoalchemist] [#1904 \xB7 2025-04-29 \xB7 econoalchemist]\n- That stratum URL served as the Telehash 2025 endpoint (event ran 2025-05-05); username doubles as your leaderboard name [#1937 \xB7 2025-04-30 \xB7 econoalchemist] [#2056 \xB7 2025-05-05 \xB7 Karl] [#2058 \xB7 2025-05-05 \xB7 szarka]\n- Signet-instead-of-mainnet bug (October 2025): a release deserialized the wrong genesis ('Invalid genesis block data' panic); root cause was looking for Signet data instead of mainnet; fixed within days [#3142 \xB7 2025-10-22 \xB7 Bartholomew] [#3143 \xB7 2025-10-22 \xB7 econoalchemist]\n- Released on October 26 (2025) and at v1.1.18 by late November ([newsletter, Nov 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-11-24-assembling-freedom-11.md)); Linux/Mac/Windows packages cut for one-step installs (October 2025) [#3151 \xB7 2025-10-28 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- v2.0.1 released for self-hosters (2026-01-07); step-by-step build instructions for the 256F server at hydrapool.org [#4169 \xB7 2026-01-07 \xB7 econoalchemist] [#4171 \xB7 2026-01-07 \xB7 econoalchemist]\n- POD256 #101 (2026-01-14): solo, PPLNS and multi-address coinbase payouts, scalability-tested to 10,000 workers; HashDash visualizes hashrate, workers and share distribution ([newsletter, Jan 2026](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2026-01-14-assembling-freedom-13.md)).\n- v2.4.0 pushed and used for the February 2026 telehash [#4427 \xB7 2026-02-16 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- devpool (rkuester): a fun remix of Hydrapool's container image for tinkering with Mujina, Bitaxe etc. [#3726 \xB7 2025-11-29 \xB7 Ryan]\n- Operation Bitcoin runs a Hydrapool instance at hydrapool.operationbitcoin.io plus an open-source ob-telehash-dashboard [#5018 \xB7 2026-07-01 \xB7 Gabe Lord] [#5048 \xB7 2026-07-17 \xB7 Nickamoto]\n- 2026-08-10 (POD256 #123, BIP 110 fork week): Eco pitched exit routes from the big pools \u2014 Parasite Pool and GridPool, plus \"We've made spinning up your own pool very easy with Hydro Pool\", adding \"I would love to see somebody take that and gain some traction and have several miners using it\" on an easy-to-deploy PPLNS model ([POD256 e123](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-08-10-e123.md)).\n\n### Event performance\n\n| Event | Pool build | Result | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Telehash #2 (2025-05-05, Austin) | ckpool fork | ~nine restarts early; peak roughly 245 Ph/s and ~2,500 workers (incl. a parallel Public Pool instance at roughly 120 Ph/s); no block | [newsletter, Jun 2025](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2025-06-19-you-know-i-m-something-of-a-decentralized-pool-myself.md) |\n| First outing of the Rust pool (posted 2026-01-24) | January 2026 performance releases (see timeline) | 1.4EH/s and 1500 peak worker connections | [jungly on X](../../raw/history/256f-research/2026-10-07-x-notable-jungly-hydrapool.md) |\n| Telehash #4 (2026-05-19, Bitcoin Park Austin) | Rust pool on an AMD EPYC 9R45 AWS EC2 instance | Peak 2.49 EH/s, average 1.32 EH/s, ~30.81 ZH total work over 6.5 hours; 2231 unique workers, 59 users; 20,754,816 accepted vs 41,377 rejected shares (0.199%); >2,000 simultaneous connections at ~1% CPU; no block | [Telehash #4 metrics](../../raw/history/256f-org/2026-05-21-forum-telehash-4-performance-metrics.md); [newsletter, May 2026](../../raw/history/256f-newsletter/2026-05-28-assembling-freedom-27.md); [jungly on X](../../raw/history/256f-research/2026-10-07-x-notable-jungly-hydrapool.md) |\n\nThe scalability-test issue (target: 1000 stratum clients) was closed on 2026-03-19 after optimizations made for the January telehash ([issue #5](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-04-09-github-hydrapool-issue-5-scalability-test-for-hydrapool-setu.md)). Telehash #4 hashrate was concentrated: the top contributor supplied 73.5959% of hashes ([Telehash #4 metrics](../../raw/history/256f-org/2026-05-21-forum-telehash-4-performance-metrics.md)). Which pool found Telehash #1's block 881423 is disputed \u2014 see [Solo Blocks Found by Open-Source & Home Miners](../history/open-source-solo-blocks.md).\n\n#### Telehash #4 stress test, as reported on POD256 e115 (2026-05-27)\n\n- Telehash #4 peaked at about a quarter percent of network hashrate on Hydrapool, almost three times any previous Telehash ([POD256 e115](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-05-27-e115.md)).\n- Metrics summarized from Hydrapool's Prometheus/Grafana backend over the six and a half hours live: about 30.8 zettahashes total, average 1.32 exahash per second, peak 2.495 exahash per second, 2,231 unique workers, 59 unique users (same source). Around 2,000 of the workers were Electron Energy, which supported a full exahash for the whole event and produced the best share, 5,600,000,000,000 (leaderboard top difficulty 5,640,000,000,000) (same source).\n- Average rejection rate was 0.19%, against a Slush Pool/Braiins rule of thumb that under 2% is acceptable (same source); the forum metrics give 0.199% (table above).\n- Jungly reported the cloud server's CPU load at about 1% with over 2,000 connections \u2014 heavily over-provisioned (same source).\n- The bottleneck was the HashDash website, not the Stratum server: API rate limits in the first thirty minutes were fixed by a caching proxy Schnitzel built on the fly; the dashboard served 452,062 total API requests at a peak of 33 requests per second, and 157,937 stream connections were logged (same source).\n- Starting difficulty: the pool parsed the otherwise-unused Stratum password field \u2014 `d` (starting difficulty) and the new `h` (miner-stated hashrate, from which the pool computes starting difficulty); vardiff still converges afterwards. Scott's rationale: too low and a big miner or proxy floods the pool with shares; too high and small miners like Bitaxes may go hours without a share and be disconnected for silence (same source). The hosts floated weight-class leaderboards keyed on `h`, with vardiff as a check on people faking a low hashrate (same source).\n\n> **Status: Disputed** \u2014 Telehash #2 peak hashrate\n> The June 2025 newsletter puts the Telehash #2 peak at roughly 245 Ph/s on Hydrapool plus roughly 120 Ph/s on a parallel Public Pool instance (table above). On POD256 e115 a host recalled the event \"floating around\" 400 petahash (machine transcription), with Public Pool running a tandem event to the same address ([POD256 e115](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-05-27-e115.md)). Best assessment: the newsletter's contemporaneous figures are more reliable than a year-later recollection; the two roughly agree if the recollection is of the combined total.\n\n#### Load testing and fractional difficulty\n\n- Before Telehash #3 the plan was to ramp up to 10,000 CPU-miner connections against the Optiplex-hosted Hydrapool server and Bitcoin Park router; at Telehash 1 there had been only about 300 to 400 unique workers despite over an exahash, implying proxies aggregating many machines ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)).\n- Load-testing with many cloud CPU miners running Mujina (which generate the same pool load per work request as ASICs) produced HashScope and led Hydrapool to support fractional difficulty below one ([POD256 e107](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-03-11-e107.md)); Ryan's classroom fork also implements fractional share difficulty with correct accounting ([POD256 e118](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-06-24-e118.md)).\n\n## Live instances (observed 2026-10-07)\n\n| Instance | Notes | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| pool.256foundation.org:3333 (256 Foundation) | Any vanity username or Nostr npub; pays 100% to the Foundation if it finds a block. Web root redirects to dash.256f.org; `/api/v1/query` serves Prometheus without auth. At 2026-10-07T21:22:40Z: 76.14 TH/s from 18 addresses and 45 workers | [hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/hydrapool-org-home.md); [redirect](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-10-07-pool-256foundation-html-redirect.md); [operator query](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-10-07-pool-256foundation-operator.md) |\n| test.hydrapool.org | README's mainnet test instance; serves a Grafana login (appUrl grafana.p2poolv2.org), no public Hydrapool Prometheus API | [test.hydrapool.org](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-10-07-test-hydrapool-org.md) |\n| CleanPool (operator Caylon, announced 2026-07-26) | Third-party PPLNS instance: first 1 BTC of its first block funds buildout, then a flat fee; 2.1% of each block to the 256 Foundation (operator claims, not checked on-chain). Templates from a Knots node with `datacarriersize=83`, `rejectparasites=1`, `rejecttokens=1`, `permitbaremultisig=0`. At 2026-10-07T21:33:30Z: 55.76 TH/s, 8 addresses, 12 workers | [CleanPool announcement](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-10-07-cleanpool-caylon.md); [CleanPool page and Prometheus](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-10-07-cleanpool-payouts-filters-prom.md) |\n| hydrapool.operationbitcoin.io | Operation Bitcoin instance | [#5018 \xB7 2026-07-01 \xB7 Gabe Lord] |\n\n### The 256 Foundation instance over time (POD256)\n\n- **Plan (late 2025).** Eco planned a self-hosted 256 Foundation Hydrapool where 100% of any block reward goes to the Foundation, migrating show hashrate contributors from other pools; worker names stay visible on the dashboard ([POD256 e094](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-12-e094.md)). He planned two instances on identical Optiplex machines: the Foundation instance hard-coded to pay 100% of a found block to the Foundation, and a Bitcoin Park members' pool paying proportionally to contributed hashrate ([POD256 e099](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-17-e099.md)).\n- **Not a business.** The Foundation does not intend to be a standing pool operator ([POD256 e088](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-09-24-e088.md); [POD256 e097](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-03-e097.md)); its instance is a fundraiser, not for earning sats, and anyone can run their own instance and control the coinbase split ([POD256 e100](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-12-31-e100.md); [POD256 e104](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-02-11-e104.md)). It became the official way to donate hashrate to the show in February 2026 ([POD256 e105](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-02-18-e105.md)), after the Foundation moved its donation hashrate off mostly-FPPS accounts ([POD256 e103](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-02-04-e103.md)).\n- **Readings on air** (each as of its episode date): 33 workers, with agent P leading at ninety five terahash ([POD256 e103](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-02-04-e103.md)); 46 workers ([POD256 e107](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-03-11-e107.md)); 19 users and 42 workers ([POD256 e110](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-01-e110.md)); 73.5 terahash, peaking near a 130 terahash in the prior twelve hours ([POD256 e111](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-10-e111.md)); from about a 100 terahash to about half a petahash in one morning, with Exergy leading ([POD256 e113](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-22-e113.md)); 287 terahash after Telehash #4 ([POD256 e115](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-05-27-e115.md)); three forty terahash at a morning peak ([POD256 e121](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-07-27-e121.md)); a weekly peak of four thirty terahash thanks to RY3T pointing Mujina-based solar-miner dev systems at it \u2014 the hosts estimated a one in two chance of the pool hitting a block before 2140 ([POD256 e126](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-09-21-e126.md)).\n- **No mainnet block yet.** As of April 2026 Hydrapool had not found a mainnet block ([POD256 e112](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-15-e112.md)).\n- **Test instance.** test.hydrapool.org on port thirty three thirty three showed PPLNS distribution by address without sign-in ([POD256 e092](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-10-29-e092.md)); Hardest Blocks pointed about half a Petahash (a spike to 500 terahash) at it and planned to self-host ([POD256 e093](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-05-e093.md)); at one recording the test dashboard showed nine workers and a best difficulty of about 3.47 billion ([POD256 e094](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-12-e094.md)).\n\n## HashDash and leaderboards\n\n- **What it is.** D++ (Dee) built HashDash because the Grafana visualizer only runs locally; it shows five-minute and one-hour hashrate (matching Grafana), an eight-hour history for the Telehash window, pool best share, per-worker best share on hover and an idle-worker toggle. It is plain browser JavaScript/jQuery, and every Hydrapool operator is meant to be able to run their own ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)). It runs at dash.256f.org ([POD256 e102](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-30-e102.md)).\n- **Identity.** Worker names can be a website, Nostr profile or Twitter handle that gets linked on the dashboard, ranked by hashrate ([POD256 e102](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-30-e102.md)). Use a Nostr npub plus a dot and worker name to pull profile info; websites work by replacing dots with dashes; badges go to the top three difficulties ([POD256 e109](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-03-25-e109.md)); emojis in Nostr names render ([POD256 e110](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-01-e110.md)).\n- **Blocks-found counter.** Scans the chain for payouts to the donation address and is hard-coded minus one to hide the first Telehash block ([POD256 e111](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-10-e111.md)).\n- **Leaderboards.** A difficulty leaderboard (best share 240,260,000 in early February 2026, [POD256 e103](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-02-04-e103.md)) and a \"loyalty\" leaderboard summing total hashes so intermittent miners cannot game rankings ([POD256 e114](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-05-13-e114.md)). Loyalty resets after a week of inactivity ([POD256 e115](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-05-27-e115.md); [POD256 e122](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-08-03-e122.md)); open questions include how resets should treat intermittent heating loads ([POD256 e114](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-05-13-e114.md)). By September 2026 the boards showed the top 20 or so ([POD256 e126](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-09-21-e126.md)). The Telehash #4 difficulty record of 5,640,000,000,000 still topped the board in August ([POD256 e122](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-08-03-e122.md)); the difficulty top three were Electron, a Wrigley block party and Megawatt, which found the Foundation's first block ([POD256 e118](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-06-24-e118.md)).\n- **September 2026 readings.** On the hashrate ranking Zarka led \"at 15.4 terahash, so it doesn't take a whole lot to be first\"; Eco was in fourth \"with 7.8 terahash\", Tyler sat around 4.6, with Scott \"Just under a terahash with the Park Austin hydro axe\". On loyalty, Eco noted Schnitzel's lead included hashrate he \"had rented over an Exahash for a period of time during our last telehash fundraiser\", and restated the reset rule: \"it gets reset if you have a seven day window where you submit zero hashes\" ([POD256 e126](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-09-21-e126.md)).\n- **Teledash.** For Telehash #3 the plan was four dashboards \u2014 a stream overlay (\"Teledash\", via OBS) with total hashrate, active workers, odds, funds raised against a goal and donation QR codes, a jumbotron, low-latency chat, and HashDash ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)).\n- **Agents.** A \"256PodAgent\" on the Foundation's Buzz relay preps show notes and summarizes HashDash activity from the Hydrapool Prometheus/Grafana logs ([POD256 e125](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-09-07-e125.md)).\n\n## Spin-offs and derived builds (POD256)\n\n- **HashScope.** Schnitzel built it during his Libre Board grant to watch traffic between the control board and the Hydrapool instance and verify shares ([POD256 e102](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-30-e102.md)); see [HashScope](../mining-software/hashscope.md).\n- **Full-stack demo (Telehash #3, January 2026).** Three Ember One hashboards on a Libre Board prototype running Mujina, pointed at Hydrapool ([POD256 e102](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-30-e102.md)).\n- **Dev Pool classroom.** Ryan's Docker Compose stack (Bitcoin node on a private classroom signet, Hydrapool, a mempool block explorer and a modified copy of Dee's dashboard) on a local Wi-Fi access point. Because signet lacks adjustable network difficulty, Ryan added a layer in Hydrapool enforcing his own network difficulty before submitting blocks, retargeting about every 10 blocks for roughly thirty-second block times; blocks paid 50 Bitcoin (signet) ([POD256 e118](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-06-24-e118.md)). Ryan also hashes his Mujina dev setup to his own Hydrapool instance ([POD256 e095](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2025/2025-11-19-e095.md)).\n- **Doom Axe.** Schnitzel's battery-powered box runs Mujina and Hydrapool plus a full node on a LibreBoard, with a Bitaxe as the hashing device and Dee's dashboard on a screen ([POD256 e114](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-05-13-e114.md)); Libre Board's design goals include running a full node and Hydrapool ([POD256 e113](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-04-22-e113.md)).\n\n\nThe metric `worker_shares_valid_total` is difficulty-weighted, so its 5-minute rate reads as H/s ([operator query](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-10-07-pool-256foundation-operator.md)). POD256 #101 show notes give the telehash endpoint as pool.256foundation.org:33303 ([POD256 #101](../../raw/history/pod256/2026-01-14-pod256-101-hydrapool-hashdash-and-the-telehash-playbook-open.md)); every other source uses port 3333, so treat 33303 as a typo.\n\n## Packaging for home-node platforms\n\n- Start9: PR #33 (opened 2025-11-06) is still open; the maintainer wants Start9 and Umbrel packages to wrap the released ghcr images rather than carry separate Dockerfiles, and to avoid a hard Nix dependency; the contributor dropped the Nix parts by 2026-02-14 ([PR #33](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2025-11-06-github-hydrapool-pr-33-add-start9-package.md)). NebulaMiner opened the PRs (November 2025) [#3323 \xB7 2025-11-14 \xB7 NebulaMiner].\n- Umbrel: on 2026-07-27 a community member got Hydrapool running as a native Umbrel app mining with a Bitaxe; jungly and econoalchemist asked for a PR so the images could be built into the release tooling ([Umbrel forum thread](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-07-27-forum-hydrapool-on-umbrel.md)) [#5254 \xB7 2026-07-27 \xB7 256F Forum].\n- Localization: PR #39 (opened 2026-01-31) proposes Chinese (zh-CN) Grafana dashboards and README ([PR #39](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/2026-01-31-github-hydrapool-pr-39-feat-add-chinese-zh-cn-localization.md)).\n\n## Auditable hashrate & the pool-signature debate\n\n- Pool signature is a 16-byte config option (default \"hydrapool\"); operators rebrand instances by editing config [#3866 \xB7 2025-12-09 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#3867 \xB7 2025-12-09 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#3869 \xB7 2025-12-09 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- Pool anonymity: 'leave it blank to not identify yourself' (like ckpool) \u2014 pool tags originally existed to advertise hashrate; counterpoint raised that reported pool hashrate isn't auditable (only Braiins tried making it feasible) [#4436 \xB7 2026-02-17 \xB7 Boots Stribling] [#4437 \xB7 2026-02-17 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}] [#4438 \xB7 2026-02-17 \xB7 Steve Barbour]\n- Hydrapool's answer: an optional public API endpoint letting anyone download all shares and audit the pool [#4443 \xB7 2026-02-17 \xB7 jungly \u{1F415}\u{1F4B8}]\n- mempool.space policy: a pool must solve one mainnet block before mempool.space will display its scriptsig; The Space's pool block appeared via Ocean/DATUM because Mechanic contributed Ocean-specific code to mempool \u2014 call to generalize it for Hydrapool and standardize [#3728 \xB7 2025-11-29 \xB7 Ryan] [#3730 \xB7 2025-11-29 \xB7 Ryan] [#3732 \xB7 2025-11-29 \xB7 Ryan] [#3733 \xB7 2025-11-29 \xB7 Ryan] [#3734 \xB7 2025-11-29 \xB7 Ryan] [#3741 \xB7 2025-11-29 \xB7 Skot Bitaxe]\n\n## Contradictions & Open Questions\n\n- **100 users: limit or default?** See the Disputed block under blockmaxweight.\n- **Platforms.** README advertises Windows/MacOS binaries; changelog removed them in 2.5.3 ([README](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-256foundation-hydrapool.md); [CHANGELOG](../../raw/pools/hydrapool/github-hydrapool-changelog.md)).\n- **Dates.** Changelog and GitHub publish dates disagree for several 2.x releases; the Telegram announcement of v2.0.1 for self-hosters came on 2026-01-07 [#4169 \xB7 2026-01-07 \xB7 econoalchemist]. The changelog also lists two entries headed `[2.5.9] - 2026-06-1222` (sic).\n- **Open bug.** Whether issue #44 affects mainnet operators running the official binary in PPLNS mode is unconfirmed by the maintainers as of 2026-10-10.\n\n- **License wording.** Hosts said Mujina and Hydrapool are licensed \"GPL v three\" ([POD256 e105](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-02-18-e105.md)); the README specifies AGPLv3, which is the controlling text.\n- **Prometheus retention.** About seven days ([POD256 e101](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-01-14-e101.md)) vs about fifteen days ([POD256 e125](../../raw/history/pod256/transcripts/2026-10-07/transcripts/2026/2026-09-07-e125.md)) \u2014 both on-air recollections, likely different configurations.\n- **Telehash #2 peak.** See the Disputed block under Event performance.\n- **Ansible.** The CHANGELOG deprecated Ansible templates in 1.1.14, while POD256 (2026-02 \u2192 2026-04) describes a new Ansible one-click AWS deploy by Schnitzel; whether that lives in the main repo is unconfirmed.\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Pool Payout Schemes](pool-payout-schemes.md)\n- [Pool Choice for Heat Miners](pool-choice-for-heat-miners.md)\n- [Public Pool](public-pool.md)\n- [Pool Centralization & FPPS](pool-centralization-and-fpps.md)\n- [Decentralized Pool Designs](../protocols/decentralized-pool-designs.md)\n- [Stratum in Practice](../protocols/stratum-in-practice.md)\n- [Solo Blocks Found by Open-Source & Home Miners](../history/open-source-solo-blocks.md)\n- [256 Foundation Community Timeline](../history/256f-community-timeline.md)\n- [Mujina](../firmware/mujina.md)\n- [asic-rs and the Fleet-Tooling Stack](../mining-software/asic-rs.md)"
